WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.330
I asked an agent what

2
00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:04.710
class 9Z has tomorrow.

3
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.560
There is no class 9Z in our school!

4
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.080
It still looked something up

5
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.300
and answered politely.

6
00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:16.330
So what did it actually do?

7
00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.050
Today: how to check what an agent

8
00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:22.150
did, before you trust it.

9
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.100
Here’s the call Darsi Tiny wrote.

10
00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:30.490
Look closely: the class says 9C, not 9Z.

11
00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.040
It has seen names like 9C far more

12
00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.140
often, so it copied the wrong one.

13
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:41.090
Small models copy names and words wrongly.

14
00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:44.800
Once, “tidy” came out as “tisums”.

15
00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:47.070
New wordings trip them up too:

16
00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:49.030
about four in five right.

17
00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:51.430
And they never say they’re unsure.

18
00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.590
So, checking an agent means

19
00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.730
comparing its call with your request.

20
00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:59.270
Is it the right tool?

21
00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.300
Did every name and number come from your

22
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.400
words, or from a tool’s result?

23
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.560
If not, stop it before it runs.

24
00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.330
Our checker has five

25
00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:12.170
rules, written in code.

26
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.900
The tool must be switched on.

27
00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.870
Details need the right shape, so

28
00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:19.590
twenty-five o’clock fails.

29
00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:23.170
Names must come from you or a result.

30
00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:26.910
No earlier step may have failed, and a

31
00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.030
person says yes to any action.

32
00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:30.860
Quick check!

33
00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.370
You asked for a reminder

34
00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:35.130
to tidy the library.

35
00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.980
The call says “tisums the library”.

36
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.270
What should happen?

37
00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:44.270
Stop it.

38
00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.980
That word isn’t in your request or in any

39
00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.360
result, so the checker stops the call.

40
00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.400
Then you ask again, in other words.

41
00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:55.650
The checker doesn’t need to be

42
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:58.010
clever: strict rules never guess.

43
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.560
Checks matter for two-step jobs too.

44
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.400
Ask for 6B’s first lesson on

45
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:05.780
Saturday, with a reminder.

46
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.620
The timetable says: no school.

47
00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.750
Darsi Tiny sets a reminder anyway, because

48
00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:14.410
it never checks a result before using it.

49
00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:17.550
A rule like this takes only a

50
00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:18.390
few lines of Python.

51
00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:20.940
The program asks: is this

52
00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.000
detail in the request?

53
00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:25.190
If it isn’t, it stops and says why.

54
00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:27.200
You’ll fill in a check like

55
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.480
this after the video.

56
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.360
In the app, Darsi Tiny plans and a

57
00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:34.860
checker of five rules reviews each call.

58
00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.680
Send the weather request: it passes.

59
00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.920
Then ask what 9Z has tomorrow, and

60
00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.300
watch the checker stop it.

61
00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.240
Turn the checker off, and try again.

62
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:47.900
Your turn.

63
00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:50.490
Darsi Tiny wants to set a

64
00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:52.710
reminder for twenty-five o’clock.

65
00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:54.690
Which rule stops it?

66
00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:57.980
The shape rule: there’s no

67
00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:59.260
twenty-five o’clock.

68
00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:02.090
Remember: an agent can be

69
00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.190
wrong and still sound sure.

70
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.520
Check the call against your request.

71
00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:09.050
Simple rules in code stop

72
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.070
slips before anything runs.

73
00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:14.770
Try it yourself: next time an app

74
00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:16.790
does something for you, look at

75
00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:17.910
what it actually did.

76
00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.240
Did it use your words exactly?

77
00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.800
Write down one slip you find, and the

78
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.040
rule that would have caught it.
